        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Americas</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of the Little Bighorn</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">25–26 June 1876</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Along the Little Bighorn River, which the Lakota call the Greasy Grass, in the Crow Indian Reservation in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a> Territory, now Montana, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Lakota (Hunkpapa, Oglala, Minneconjou, Sans Arc, Brule and others), Dakota, Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a> and a few Arapaho, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitting-bull">Sitting Bull</a> (spiritual leader of the Hunkpapa), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crazy-horse">Crazy Horse</a>, Gall and others, with about 2,000 able-bodied fighters in the camp; against the 7th Cavalry under Lieutenant Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-armstrong-custer">George Armstrong Custer</a>, with Major Marcus Reno and Captain Frederick Benteen, and Crow and Arikara scouts. Strength figures: 700 men for the regiment, and 31 officers and 566 enlisted men under Custer on 22 June</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">Every soldier in the five companies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-armstrong-custer">Custer</a> was killed, and Reno's and Benteen's men were pinned on the bluffs until the Terry column approached on 27 June, when the Native forces withdrew. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> went on to win the war.</span></div>
        </div>
