    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Lakota, Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a> and Arapaho were not an army raised for the occasion: the village gathered to take counsel, and the 'Strongheart Society' of caretakers and providers, not a warrior class, looked after the camp.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-armstrong-custer">Custer</a>'s plan seems to have rested on taking non-combatants as hostages and on an estimate of no more than 800 'hostiles' in the area, a figure that left out the thousands who had joined from the reservations.</li><li>The Native victory did not save the Lakota's land: within months Congress cut off their rations until they ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a>, and in 1980 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Supreme Court acknowledged that the Hills were taken without just compensation.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-armstrong-custer">Custer</a>'s command the only documented and verified survivor was said to be Captain Keogh's horse, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a>, yet over 120 people came forward in the next 70 years claiming to be the 'lone survivor'.</p></aside>
    </section>

