    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1851</span><span class="rf-text">The Treaty of Fort Laramie names the area of the Little Bighorn</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1868</span><span class="rf-text">The valley becomes part of the new Crow Indian Reservation</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Early June 1876</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitting-bull">Sitting Bull</a> has a vision of soldiers falling into his camp during a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-dance">Sun Dance</a> on Rosebud <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Mid-June 1876</span><span class="rf-text">General Crook's column retreats after the Battle of the Rosebud</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Late June 1876</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-armstrong-custer">Custer</a> divides the 7th Cavalry, Reno attacks the village and is driven to the bluffs, and Custer's five companies are overwhelmed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Late June 1876</span><span class="rf-text">The fight with Reno and Benteen continues for much of the next day, and General Terry's column approaches on 27 June as the Native forces draw off</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1877</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitting-bull">Sitting Bull</a> escapes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crazy-horse">Crazy Horse</a> surrenders and the Great Sioux War ends</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-the-little-bighorn" data-v="441ffbe278" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>Reno crosses the river and charges the village</b> Reno's three troops forded the Little Bighorn at the mouth of Reno Creek on Custer's orders and rode towards the village. Custer's battalion followed the ridges to the east, while Benteen and the pack train were still behind.</li><li><b>Reno dismounts and the warriors ride out</b> Reno stopped a few hundred yards short of the village, fearing a trap, and put his men into a skirmish line. The mounted warriors streamed out and rode against the centre and exposed left end of the line.</li><li><b>The warriors turn Reno's left and he falls back to the trees</b> Over 500 warriors massed behind a small hill on Reno's left and attacked his left and rear. Reno withdrew into the timber along the river bend, where the warriors pinned his men down.</li><li><b>Reno's men break for the bluffs</b> Reno led his men out of the timber in a disorderly rout along the river. They crossed under attack and climbed the bluffs on the far side, leaving wounded behind in the timber.</li><li><b>Benteen and the pack train join Reno on the bluffs</b> Benteen's three troops reached Reno's shaken men while they were still climbing from the valley, and McDougall's troop and the pack train came later. The command dug rifle pits and held an all-round defence.</li><li><b>Yates' companies appear at the mouth of Medicine Tail Coulee</b> With Reno driven from the village, Custer moved north and a wing of his battalion under Yates went down to the river at the Minneconjou Ford. Hundreds of warriors left the fight with Reno to protect the village.</li><li><b>Custer's battalion is overwhelmed on Custer Hill</b> Custer's companies moved to Custer Hill, where the warriors, led by Crazy Horse by the Lakota account, charged from the northeast. Custer's remaining troops, E, F and half of C, were soon killed.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sitting-bull"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sitting-bull.jpg" alt="Sitting Bull" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sitting-bull">Sitting Bull</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sitting_Bull_by_D_F_Barry_ca_1883_Dakota_Territory.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">David F. Barry, Photographer, Bismarck, Dakota Territory, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle belongs to the Sioux Wars of 1854 to 1890, fought as settlers pressed onto the Great Plains. The Lakota had been displaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in and around Fort Laramie, and white encroachment into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a>, which the Lakota consider sacred, added to the pressure. Most battles of the Great Sioux War were fought on land the Lakota had taken from other peoples since 1851. The Little Bighorn valley lay within the Crow Indian Reservation, set up on 7 May 1868, and the Crow, who had a treaty claim there, had not consented to the Lakota presence. In 1873 the Crow chief Blackfoot had called for United States military action. The Crow and the Arikara became allies of the United States.</p><p>In late spring 1876 the Lakota and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a> held their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-dance">Sun Dance</a>, the most important religious event of the year, which some 'agency Indians' who had slipped away from their reservations also attended. Around 5 June, on Rosebud <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitting-bull">Sitting Bull</a> reportedly saw soldiers falling into his camp like grasshoppers from the sky. At the same time the army ran a summer campaign to force the Lakota and Cheyenne back to their reservations, using three columns: Gibbon's from Fort Ellis, Crook's from Fort Fetterman and Terry's, which included the 7th Cavalry, from Fort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a>. Crook's column retreated after the Battle of the Rosebud on 17 June, and the army's plans to converge around 26 or 27 June went awry.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Little Bighorn&#8221; cites where it covers 1876 Sun Dance ceremony, 1876 U.S. military campaign:</p><ul><li>Nathaniel Philbrick, The Last Stand: Custer, Sitting Bull, and the Battle of the Little Bighorn (Viking, 2010)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/george-armstrong-custer"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-armstrong-custer.jpg" alt="George Armstrong Custer" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/george-armstrong-custer">George Armstrong Custer</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Custer_Bvt_MG_Geo_A_1865_LC-BH831-365-crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photographer is Mathew Brady (died 1896) and his field staff, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Native camp held Lakota of several divisions (Hunkpapa, Oglala, Minneconjou, Sans Arc, Brule and others), Dakota, Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a> and five Arapaho men who had come close by accident on a hunting trip and were nearly killed by Lakota who took them for scouts until Two Moons intervened. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitting-bull">Sitting Bull</a> was the Hunkpapa spiritual leader; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crazy-horse">Crazy Horse</a>, Gall, Hump, Two Moons and many others are named among the leaders. The tribes had gathered to take counsel and did not form an army or warrior class. About 2,000 able-bodied fighters took part. They carried war clubs, lances and bows, mostly trade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>, and about 200 Winchester Model 1866 repeating rifles. Captain Weir later saw some 1,800 lodges.</p><p>The 7th Cavalry had been formed after the Civil War, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-armstrong-custer">Custer</a> commanded it as lieutenant colonel because Colonel Sturgis was on detached duty. About 20 per cent of the troopers, 139 of 718, had enlisted in the previous seven months. On 22 June Terry sent 31 officers and 566 enlisted men under Custer on a reconnaissance in force. Custer declined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gatling-gun">Gatling guns</a> and an extra battalion of the 2nd Cavalry. The troopers carried the Springfield Model 1873 carbine and Colt revolver, without sabres, and Crow and Arikara scouts went with them. The army worked from an agents' estimate of no more than 800 'hostiles' in the area, and on 25 June Custer divided the regiment into Reno's three troops, Benteen's three, five under himself and one with the pack train.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On the evening of 24 June <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-armstrong-custer">Custer</a>'s scouts reached the Crow's Nest. Told on the 25th that his trail had been found, and fearing the village would scatter, Custer decided to attack at once. Around 3:10 pm Reno crossed the river and rode towards the village, but the Lakota and Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a> were present 'in force and not running away'. Reno dismounted his men in a skirmish line, and after about 20 minutes of long-range firing more than 500 warriors turned his exposed left flank. He led a disorderly retreat through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and across the river, and reported that three officers and 29 troopers died in it. His survivors reached the bluffs, where Benteen's and later McDougall's troops joined them.</p><p>What happened to Custer's five companies is largely conjecture, since none of the men who went forward with him survived. Native accounts, Crow scouts and archaeology disagree on whether he approached the river at Medicine Tail Coulee. Lakota and Cheyenne accounts say that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crazy-horse">Crazy Horse</a> led a charge on the cavalrymen. The soldiers' resistance broke up, and most Native casualties were probably suffered on Last Stand Hill. The Lakota said the fight with Custer lasted less than an hour; David Humphreys Miller put it under half an hour. At the bluffs the fighting went on until dark, about 9:00 pm, and for much of the next day.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Little Bighorn&#8221; cites where it covers Battlefield and surrounding areas, Military assumptions prior to the battle, Reno's attack, Custer's fight:</p><ul><li>Douglas D. Scott, They Died With Custer: Soldiers' Bones from the Battle of the Little Bighorn (University of Oklahoma Press, 2013)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The army's own dead were 16 officers and 242 troopers killed or dead of wounds, with 1 officer and 51 troopers wounded, 52 per cent casualties. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-armstrong-custer">Custer</a>, his brothers Thomas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, his brother-in-law James Calhoun and his nephew Henry Reed were among the dead. Estimates of Native dead run from as few as 36, from Native lists by name, to as many as 300; Red Horse told Colonel Wood in 1877 of 136 dead and 160 wounded. A list of positively known casualties holds 99 names, consolidated to 31 identified warriors. Six women and four children were killed at the start of Reno's charge, among them two wives and three children of Gall.</p><p>Within 48 hours the camp broke into smaller groups, since there was not enough game and grass. Terry's column came up on 27 June; the Crow scout White Man Runs Him told the officers that Custer's force had been wiped out. The soldiers buried the dead in shallow graves where they had fallen. Crook and Terry waited seven weeks for reinforcements, and Miles took command in October. In May 1877 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitting-bull">Sitting Bull</a> went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crazy-horse">Crazy Horse</a> surrendered at Fort Robinson. Congress attached the 'sell or starve' rider to the 1876 Indian Appropriations Act, cutting off Sioux rations until they ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Little Bighorn&#8221; cites where it covers Aftermath:</p><ul><li>J. S. Brust, Where Custer Fell: Photographs of the Little Bighorn Battlefield Then and Now (University of Oklahoma Press, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>No man who rode with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-armstrong-custer">Custer</a>'s five companies lived to tell of their fight. What is known comes from Lakota, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a> and Crow accounts, from the Arikara and Crow scouts and from the soldiers on Reno Hill. Accounts by Red Horse, Gall, Moving Robe, Iron Hawk and Black Elk survive. In 1908 Edward Curtis walked the field with Crow scouts and then with Two Moons and Cheyenne warriors, and between 1935 and 1955 David Humphreys Miller interviewed the last Lakota survivors. Thomas Bailey Marquis gathered Native stories in the 1930s that were not published until 1976, because their claims were unpopular. In 1879 the Army held a Court of Inquiry at Reno's request.</p><p>Archaeology has changed the picture. Shell cases found in the 1920s on Nye-Cartwright Ridge and recent work on Custer Hill have led to new readings of the fight, and some now doubt that there was a 'Last Stand' as popular culture shows it. Historians still dispute Custer's route, whether he tried to cross at Medicine Tail Coulee and where the north end of the village lay. Accounts by Curley and by the trooper Peter Thompson are contested, and historians almost universally discredit Thompson's. Native casualty figures vary widely, and the weight of the weapons, such as the Springfield carbine's extractor, is debated, its part in the defeat being judged negligible on the archaeological evidence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Within weeks Congress added 2,500 men to the Army and enlarged cavalry companies. The Sioux were pressed to cede the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a>, which they never accepted as legitimate. The Supreme Court's 1980 decision in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> v. Sioux Nation of Indians acknowledged that the United States took the Hills without just compensation, and the Sioux refused the money offered and still insist on their right to the land. The Crow, who mourned in their own way, saw an end to Lakota raids; Plenty Coups recalled that his people could finally sleep without fear.</p><p>In the United States <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-armstrong-custer">Custer</a> became a hero. His widow Elizabeth Bacon Custer lived until 1933 and defended his name in three books, Captain Frederick Whittaker's 1876 book idealised him, and Wild West shows spread the image. After 1934, when Glory Hunter appeared, and her death, historians turned to criticism of his decisions. Reno's conduct was examined at the 1879 inquiry, which found him without fault, and Benteen was blamed by some for not riding to Custer. A national cemetery was created in 1879, the site was named for Custer in 1946, and in 1991 Congress renamed it Little Bighorn Battlefield National Monument. An Indian Memorial by Colleen Cutschall, 'Peace Through Unity', stands near Last Stand Hill, and red granite markers for Native warriors have been added since 1999.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On 25–26 June 1876 Lakota, Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a> and Arapaho fighters defeated the 7th Cavalry on the Little Bighorn, killing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-armstrong-custer">Custer</a> and every man in the five companies with him. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> still won the Great Sioux War, and took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a>.</em></p>

